// Default propagation models for the Java frontends (VirtualMethodTable::Java -- dex, apk, jvm,
// jar). Propagation only; CTADL ships no default sources or sinks.
//
// PARENTS. `parent`/`parents` matches the class DECLARED AT THE INVOKE SITE, not the runtime
// type. A generator listing only the interface misses every call written against a concrete
// class, and vice versa -- which is why the `iterator` entry below lists eight parents for one
// method. Every entry here lists the interface AND the common concrete types.
//
// POSITIONAL PORTS ONLY. dex and jvm name instance fields differently -- dex wraps the field's
// `pretty_name`, jvm builds `<Lclass;->field:descriptor>` -- so a named-field port is not
// portable between the two Java frontends. `Argument(n)`, `Return`, and the array element are.
//
// THE ARRAY ELEMENT is `Symbol("[]")` on both dex and jvm, and a port names it `.\[]`, ESCAPED
// -- in JSON, "Argument(0).\\[]". Unescaped, `[` at segment start introduces an offset, so
// `Argument(0).[]` is a hard load error (`InvalidOffset("")`) rather than a silent mismatch. The
// four collection generators at the end of this file used to use a synthetic `.rep` instead,
// which no frontend emits: `List.add` wrote it and `List.get` read it, so add->get chains
// composed with each other but never joined a real array write. `[]` is what the frontends
// actually emit, so they do now.
//
// A port pair is a PREFIX SUBSTITUTION, not a filter: `Argument(0).\[] -> Return` unwraps a
// level, `Argument(1) -> Argument(0).\[]` adds one. See docs/model-generators.md §7 and the
// fixtures in `tests/tnt/port_*.tnt`.
{"find":"methods","where":[{"constraint":"signature_match","name":"toString"},{"constraint":"signature","pattern":".*\\(\\)Ljava/lang/String;"}],"model":{"propagation":[{"input":"Argument(*)","output":"Return"}]}}
